- Story & Context: At the abandoned marshaling yard of Ghor-el-Rabb, caravan technician Kaveh attempted to hot-wire a scavenged analog monosynth directly into the terminal junction box to charge its dried capacitors against the freezing desert twilight. The resulting voltage spike ignited the wiring harness, sending an apocalyptic square-wave roar tearing down twenty miles of rusted iron track while the dry air choked on the stench of burning solder and scorched petroleum. As the horn section climbed atop the water crane to match the frequency of the burning engine's whistle, Kaveh screamed the night's opening curse into a battered ribbon microphone: "No train survives the salt." The collision of overdriven brass and sub-octave distortion forced the wandering tribes to fall to their knees in paralyzed adoration.
